  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Fast westerly flow is bringing in dry air across\nVermont and northern New York. Temperatures currently range in \nthe upper 60s to mid 70s. A well mixed boundary layer has \nresulted in 20-30 mph gusts across the forecast area, and even a\nfew peak gusts around 35- 40 mph. The expected cold front is \nstill off to our north. Radar shows very little activity, and \nstrong 700mb layer inversion with low to mid-level drying will \npermit only a rogue shower, if any, as the front crosses \nsoutheast. Overnight, winds will slacken, but not completely die\noff. Additionally, there will be some high clouds initially. \nThis will result in slow cooling brought on by the cold \nadvection early this evening, and then a sharper drop before \nsunrise when radiational cooling will become more dominant. \nOvernight low temperatures should settle into the lower 40s to \nlower 50s, with perhaps a spot upper 30 for the perennially cold\nAdirondack Airport.\n\nFriday will be nice and cool with beautiful weather with mid \n60s to lower 70s. Good radiational cooling will ensue overnight,\nbut surface high pressure will amble offshore, and a little bit\nof southerly return flow will follow. Another night of lower \n40s to lower 50s, and probably a few additional upper 30s in \ncolder hollows appears most likely. Saturday will be similar, \nbut warming solidly into the 70s with the southerly flow. Then \nSaturday night will be more humid with warmer overnight lows in \nthe mid 50s to mid 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Sunday will start warm and humid as a narrow \ntongue of Gulf moisture reaches the forecast area. There's a \nfairly strong upper low moving along Hudson Bay, but its \nattendant surface reflection will be in far northern Quebec \nProvince. So the cold front extending south of the surface low \nwill be quite elongated over our region. A lee side trough \nenhanced by diurnally driven convection will track along the \nMid-Atlantic. It will keep the bulk of better instability and \nmoisture away from our region, while we only have marginal \nsynoptic forcing out of the stretched out frontal boundary. \nStill, good surface heating will push temperatures into the mid \n70s to lower 80s, and this will result in about 750-1250 J/kg of\nCAPE. A few showers and thunderstorms seem reasonable, but the \nGFS simulated IR satellite doesn't hint at the potential for \nstrong updrafts with a lack of cold cloud tops. Shear does \nappear to be on the high side at 45-50 knots. Given weak \nforcing, only modest instability, and dry air entrainment, that \nshear might just be too much. We'll keep an eye, but the most \nlikely case is garden variety scattered showers and \nthunderstorms with breezy conditions ahead of the front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: 500 mb flow in the mid-high latitudes are \nfavored to be mainly zonal with Arctic air trapped well north of\nthe region. Fast- moving troughs will be moving through this \nflow with model trends shifting to a later approach of Tuesday's\nwave. As such, chances were lowered Tuesday afternoon and \nshifted later into Tuesday night/Wednesday as the trough moves \nthrough. Some models keep a stalled surface boundary over the \nregion which will have implications to temperatures and \nprecipitation chances going forward. Should this come to \nfruition, a wetter period would be possible along/south of this \nboundary. Either way, another wave is projected to move through \nThursday into the end of the work week with more chances of \nafternoon showers and maybe a rumble of thunder.",
